+<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
+<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Estimating pass@<math id="A1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math></h2>
+
+<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
+<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While all estimators mentioned previously are consistent, only the empirical estimate used by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kulal et al. (2019)</a></cite>, and (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Functional Correctness ‣ 2 Evaluation Framework ‣ Evaluating Large Language Models Trained on Code" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) are unbiased. Evaluating pass@<math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> in an unbiased way with any number of samples <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is important for fair comparison. For example, estimating <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{pass@$k$}=1-(1-\text{pass@1})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>pass@</mtext><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mtext>pass@1</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{pass@$k$}=1-(1-\text{pass@1})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-(1-\hat{p})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-(1-\hat{p})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> using the empirical pass@1, results in a consistent underestimate as shown in Figure <a href="#A1.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ Appendix A Estimating pass@𝑘 ‣ Evaluating Large Language Models Trained on Code" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>. The gap doesn’t fully close even when <math id="A1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;5k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;5k</annotation></semantics></math>, and results can seem better with more samples. The interpretation of this estimator is that we draw <math id="A1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> samples with replacement from a pool of <math id="A1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> candidates, but the <math id="A1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> samples are not independent.</p>
+</div>
+<figure id="A1.F13" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2107.03374v2/figs/estimators.png" id="A1.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/589;" width="476" height="589" alt="Refer to caption">
+<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 13: </span>Comparing the amount of bias and variance of two estimators of pass@<math id="A1.F13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. While the top expression may look correct, it underestimates the true value by a considerable margin. The unbiased estimator may have a slightly higher variance initially but allows for a fair comparison across different numbers of samples.</figcaption>
+</figure>
+<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
+<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">(<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Functional Correctness ‣ 2 Evaluation Framework ‣ Evaluating Large Language Models Trained on Code" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is unbiased, because it estimates the fail probability <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\text{pass@1})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mtext>pass@1</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\text{pass@1})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> as the probability of drawing <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> failed samples without replacement. To show this, note that <math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of correct samples that pass the unit tests, is distributed <math id="A1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Binom}(n,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Binom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Binom}(n,p)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is pass@1, and that (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Functional Correctness ‣ 2 Evaluation Framework ‣ Evaluating Large Language Models Trained on Code" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) evaluates to 1 when <math id="A1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-c&lt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-c&lt;k</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,</p>
+</div>
